  "summary": "The RTL implements the zeroize emergency-erase function using a derived signal zeroize_mask, which is then used inside the always block labeled zeroize_block to select the next value of the key register (key_q_next). Under some conditions related to bus activity, an asserted zeroize can be silently suppressed, causing key_q_r to retain stale key material instead of clearing. This appears to violate the design_brief.md contract that zeroize must force key_q to 32'b0 on the next clock edge unconditionally. The exact qualifying bus state was not conclusively isolated during this review; further simulation across various bus_wr/bus_busy timings is recommended to pin down the precise triggering window. Expected correct behavior for the top candidate: whenever zeroize=1, key_q_r should become 32'b0 on the very next clock edge.",
  "tool_version_or_method": "Manual RTL review of inputs/key_loader_ctrl.v cross-referenced against inputs/design_brief.md's stated zeroize priority contract; limited simulation using iverilog/vvp was performed but did not conclusively pin down the exact bus signal combination responsible for the intermittent zeroize failure."
}

